// Optional value: `option[T]` is either a `T` or absent. Constructed by the
// compiler; T is the wrapped type.
#[_builtin(option)]
struct option[T any] {}

// Mutable reference cell: `ref[T]` is a handle to a `T` rather than a copy of
// one, so what holds it goes on seeing what that cell becomes. T is the referent
// type. `&x` makes one and `*r` reads through it.
//
// A parameter declared `ref[T]` also takes a plain `T`, and then it is the value
// it always was — which is what lets one declaration serve a caller who wants
// the handle and one who wants the contents, `f(&x)` against `f(x)`. Permitted
// only where the callee does nothing with the parameter but read through it: a
// value has no cell to write back to, or to outlive the call in.
//
// NoLambda's lowering uses one for a mutable capture, which is where the type
// came from.
#[_builtin(ref)]
struct ref[T any] {}

// Read-only value: `readonly[T]` is a `T` that may be read and not written --
// neither assigned, nor toggled, nor written through a field or an element,
// nor handed to a mutating method such as `push`. It is the type of a loop
// variable bound by copy: `for var x = xs` gives `x` a `readonly[T]`, since
// a write to a copy would change nothing read again, and `for var &x = xs` is
// the binding that writes the element. The index of a loop, and a map's key and
// value, are read-only too.
//
// The compiler gives the type; a program may not write it.
#[_builtin(readonly)]
struct readonly[T any] {}

// The absence of a value. Assignable to any `option[T]`, and what a bare
// `option[T]` holds until one is given.
//
// The written value is a placeholder the compiler replaces: `null` has no
// literal to be declared with, and its type is not one that can be spelled.
#[_builtin(null)]
const null = 0
